Subject: Report the size and alignment each type was laid out to

bindgen knows how big every type it writes is: it reads the size and alignment
clang measured, pads the struct out to that size, and gives it that alignment.
`--layout-tests` writes the two numbers into the bindings as an assertion, and
that is the only place they appear. The switch is one switch over the whole
output - there is no asking for the assertions on some types and not others -
and a consumer which cannot use it has no way to learn the numbers at all.

A consumer which renders the same C++ objects a second way needs them. Code
which constructs a C++ object in storage a Rust caller owns has to size that
storage, and the two renderings agreeing is the whole of what makes the write
land inside it; nothing checks that today, and where they disagree the
disagreement is silent. Report the layout through `ParseCallbacks`, for each
struct, union and enum codegen writes out.

The number reported is the one bindgen laid the type out to, which is what
clang measured for all but a union whose members clang could not lay out:
`CompInfo::layout` answers for one of those with the widest member it can
measure, without rounding up to the alignment, and that is what bindgen writes
as well as what it reports.

Reported after the bail-out for a class with non-type template parameters, so
that a layout reported is a layout something was written to. Not reported at
all for a type with no layout, which is so for a forward declaration and for
most of what a class template's own pattern can be.
